+A row is "reserved" from the moment the shop decides to make the refund, "sent" once the processor
+has told a worker it accepted it, and "failed" only when the shop knows the request never left it.
+Reserved and sent rows are both money this payment has already promised back. A sent one is
+promised because the processor pays an accepted refund out on its next settlement run whether or
+not anyone asks again, from the moment it is accepted rather than from the moment it settles. A
+reserved one is promised because the request is on the wire, or was on the wire and the answer
+never came home: for as long as the shop cannot say the processor refused it, that money may
+already be going back, and a second worker asking in the meantime must not be offered it too.
+
+A reserved row is also unfinished business rather than a licence. It belongs to the one worker that
+took it, and that worker is named on the row by the reservation this book hands out with it. Only
+that worker, still holding that reservation, may end the row; a second worker that meets the row
+has to find out what became of it rather than act on it, because the room it is holding may be
+released or spent the moment its owner comes back, and two workers acting on one reservation is
+how a payment gives back twice what a single click asked for.
+
+Only a failed row frees its amount again, and a row is failed only for the one ending that is not
+ambiguous: the connection turned the request away before it went. A row whose answer was lost stays
+reserved, because the request may be sitting at the processor waiting for the nightly run. It is
+the processor, asked about the reference the refund was sent under, that ends that row.
+
+A failed row is not the end of the click either. The agent presses refund again and the row asks
+for its money a second time, so `reserve` measures it against the remainder as it stands at that
+moment rather than handing back the room it had before, and hands out a fresh reservation for the
+worker that wins it. The reservation the row carried before is spent: whoever was holding it has
+been superseded and can no longer end this row.
+
+Rows are kept under the click that made them, and a click stands for one amount on one payment.
+The same click coming back for a different amount, or against a different payment, is not that
+click and this book will not answer for it.
